ite often you would actually need a  response from the from the server itself  to indicate that the port is in fact  open and you might sound like an ICMP  requests at it  so moving on in this case we actually  ended up using the sink package so  before we had used run groups which are  based on the sink package now we're  using the sink package itself so how do  we build a TCP port scanner this is  actually even simpler than our TCP proxy  because all we're doing is picking a  range of ports we're just trying to open  and connect to one of those remote  addresses and then we're printing our  results if we have an error and we can't  actually establish a connection we're  just going to assume that it's the port  is closed so step one same thing as  before actually very similar code the  only difference is now we're actually  having adding a timeout so we're just  trying to open and connect a TCP socket  to a remote address and port and if it's  closed we're printing that out if it's  open and we succeed  we're also printing that out and we're  making sure we close the connection the  other thing is of course we could do  this all sequentially but it would make  more sense to do this concurrently so we  are using wait groups a little bit  different from the run groups from  before whereas those run groups and  start after  routines at the same time the weight  groups will actually cause the main  thread to block while our sub go  routines are executing and something  else that's kind of cool which is a  pattern that I've come to use a lot is  using a buffered channel which you can  kind of see at the top we're making a  channel with an empty struck that  happens to have a size of ten so it uses  this construct a lot to kind of limit  the number of concurrent go routines  that I can have in a service so in this  case we start our go routine we're  adding something to the channel so it  fills up when we're done with the go  routine we're reading from the channel  so it