o the server it will ultimately  be having our source MAC address from  our DHCP client as well as the IP  address that we're getting what we  wanted to do was confirm that the source  MAC address that's in the body of the  DHCP request happens to be the same as  that source MAC address that is in the  header of our Ethernet frame this would  allow us to kind of detect if a certain  DHCP clients were spoofing behavior and  detect bad actors so to build a decency  HTTP server however now we're finally  dropping down and we're using those raw  sockets or specifically packet sockets  so we used a library to open and find a  raw socket to an interface then we  essentially read from that interface we  and marshaled that DHCP message and then  we tried appending on the type of DHCP  message we had we had different handlers  we finally were to validate the DHCP  message if it happened to be of the  request type finally we would craft a  response and send that response back to  our sender so one of the packages that  we used for this is something that my  colleague actually previously wrote Matt  layer called the raw package if we kind  of dive into the raw package we can see  that on Linux systems he's actually  using packet sockets essentially opening  a socket binding it to the interface and  then reading from the socket on FreeBSD  however he's using the beep BPF  interfaces which I had mentioned earlier  something you'll also notice in both of  these code examples is that we're using  a bunch of other packages from the  standard library we're using OS we're  using syscall and then we're also using  this other package called Unix in order  to open our sockets  so I also want to highlight that quite  frequently and this is what Matt ended  up doing because the NGO standard  library and especially than that package  didn't really have exactly what he  needed for packet sockets he created his  own library using syscall and OS and the  UNIX package which happens to also use  this call and OS under the hood so in  order to do this all we're doing is  opening and binding a raw socket to the  interface something else I want to point  out is we're creating this socket and  binding it to the interface we are  specifying the ethertype and header so  here we're saying ethertype ipv4 meaning  that we're expecting the ipv4 IP v4  protocol type data to come over this raw  socket and so technically this code  snippet was actually from RG h CP v4  server so we were expecting ipv4  messages second Lane once we have that  socket open we would read incoming data  into a buffer on a package that pass it  into a channel have one of our workers  in our channels handling that request  and here you can kind of see we're using  that that same idea of a buffered  Channel and a limited number of workers  to serve our requests same idea that we  were using before when we created the  port scanner so finally this is where  we're doing that validation we're  actually reading our DHCP packet that's  been pushed into another type of struct  but we're also comparing that to what we  have in our packet or in our frame  header that source MAC address and if in  fact they're not equal we're just  sending an invalid message back to our  client otherwise if it's not invalid  we'll just send back a happy response so  the the main I mean obviously the the  full production ready DHCP server was  way more code than this but for the sake  of this example I just wanted to point  out two main things one we're using raw  sockets and two we decided to use raw  sockets and jump down to the link layer  because we wanted to do source Mac  validation so that leads me to my  conclusion so if there's anything  learned from all of this it's that go  can be good for building networking  primitives is it always the language you  want to use not necessarily but it can  be good and then when we're discussing  layers for layer seven we have the net  HTTP package within the standard library  if we're jumping down further we have  the net package and if we're jumping  down even further we have the raw  package but we can also use the OS  package or the syscall package or use  other third-party packages to give us  access to that link layer data that we  need and even outside like outside of  networking there's a lot of cool  features and go when it comes to  concurrency primitives with the sync  package error groups weight groups like  all of these ideas of actor go routines  being able to be started or terminated  as well as goroutines and channels so  thank you and before I end I just wanted  to thank some of my colleagues that  layer who actually ended up writing the  raw package that we used within DHCP my  other colleague Nick and then julius who  happened to pear program with me on a  lot of different networking primitives  so thanks if you have any questions  yeah that's a good point  we did have to add we did have to have  root permissions or specific like user  groups in order to do this but they were  running as hypervisor level daemons so  we had that permission we preferred it  for pretty much most of our networking  services that are running on the  hypervisor we do have root permission  and the right bra capabilities added to  the groups yep  yeah so I would say that I think it had  a lot to do with the growth of the  company and the products that were being  created and then being able to have the  right velocity to kind of diverge into  separate pillars and separate teams so  we've started I think moving over to  using kubernetes and the micro services  when I joined the company and before I  think and this is true for a lot of  companies when they create their first  product or their MVP they might use  something like Ruby or rails or  what-have-you to create it just because  it's really fast to work in but then at  a certain point it becomes too big and  then there's also a ton of latency  that's introduced so I think it was the  com